# Findings `just doctor` should report as accepted rather than as news.
#
# The doctor's counterpart to .lint-baseline, and deliberately not the same
# shape. .lint-baseline is a count, which works for shellcheck because any
# increase fails. Doctor's findings are qualitative and differ per machine, so
# a count would hide a new finding the moment an old one was fixed. This is an
# accept-list keyed on identity (SI-126).
#
# FORMAT — check|host|glob|reason
#
#   check   the doctor check the finding comes from
#   host    a hostname, or "*" for every host
#   glob    matched against the finding text with shell globbing
#   reason  why it is accepted, and the ticket. Mandatory; `just lint` fails a
#           row without one.
#
# An inline "#" is NOT a comment here, so a reason can name a PR or an issue.
# Only a line whose first non-blank character is "#" is a comment.
#
# WHAT BELONGS HERE, AND WHAT DOES NOT
#
# A row says "this is true, we know, and no action follows". It is the wrong
# answer for anything that is merely inconvenient, unfinished, or someone
# else's turn — those are findings, and the backlog is where they belong.
#
# Two failure modes this file has to avoid, and the guards against each:
#
#   1. Silence. An accepted finding is still printed under --verbose with its
#      reason, and the count is named in the summary on every run, including a
#      clean one. Green means "nothing unexpected", never "nothing".
#   2. Rot. A row that matches nothing is reported as a finding of its own, so
#      an acceptance cannot outlive the thing it accepted. Delete it when the
#      doctor tells you to.
#
# The rows are host-scoped for the same reason bin/lib/config-owners.manifest
# is: three machines whose differences are permanent, and an acceptance written
# for one of them should not quietly cover the others.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Three node providers is the standing SI-86 item. volta is in all three
# Brewfiles, in shell/env.spec and in shell/path.spec, so it is present on every
# machine; the mise shims come first, so mise wins. Unwinding it is a migration
# across three machines rather than an edit, and check_runtimes says so in a
# comment above itself. Accepted until someone does the migration — at which
# point this row starts matching nothing and the doctor will say so.
runtimes|*|node has * providers:*|volta is deliberately not being unwound — SI-86, and the comment above check_runtimes

# Not a defect at all. Numbers appears exactly once per host; the two ids are a
# cross-host split, because macbook-2019 runs the 14.x releases under one App
# Store listing while the other two run 15.x under an older one. Established in
# SI-86 and written up in docs/architecture.md. The brewfile analyser compares
# names across hosts and cannot tell a genuine product split from a data bug.
brewfile|*|Brewfile inconsistency: mas "Numbers" has several ids*|two real App Store listings for one product, not a duplicate — SI-86 and docs/architecture.md

# Pages has the same cross-host App Store split as Numbers. The remaining
# same-id findings are display-name drift in `mas list`; Brew Bundle identifies
# these apps by id, so reinstalling them only to align the generated labels
# would not change what gets restored.
brewfile|*|Brewfile inconsistency: mas "Pages" has several ids*|two real App Store listings for one product, not a duplicate — docs/architecture.md
brewfile|*|Brewfile inconsistency: mas id 1474276998 has several names*|same App Store app id; only the generated display name differs — docs/architecture.md
